findings=[
 {'id':'Q214','title':'Resident hint persistence can overwrite successful pair and unpair changes despite single-writer and atomic-update claims',
  'source':'ports/djinn/src/pairing.rs:pair_device/unpair_device; personal_sync.rs:refresh_dial_hint; settings.rs:OwnerSettings::save',
  'status':'two native public read/write interleavings reproduced; documented, not repaired',
  'evidence':'pairing.rs says the resident never writes settings and that management pair/unpair are the only writers. refresh_dial_hint also loads, changes and saves the whole OwnerSettings file, claiming its atomic path avoids clobbering concurrent pair/unpair. The save and load sequence has no cross-process lock or revision check. A public unpair_device call returns Removed and removes the device; saving an earlier hint snapshot then restores both the paired device and its roster root. A public pair_device call returns Added; saving an earlier hint snapshot then removes that new device and root. Fresh-read controls preserve both management changes.',
  'scope':'Explicitly ordered public API operations reproduce the possible read/write interleaving and final file contents. The fixture follows the resident hint load/record_endpoint/save critical section and uses real pair/unpair APIs. No timed race between resident processes, live roster reconciliation, key re-admission, peer connection or user settings mutation was executed.',
  'repair_triage':{'disposition':'documented in read-only audit','size':'bounded shared settings transaction seam','reason':'Make all management and hint writers use the same cross-process load-modify-commit owner, or move live management through the resident. Locking only the final rename does not protect an earlier snapshot. Preserve independent concurrent fields and separately address crash-safe replacement; do not claim a rename is an atomic read-modify-write transaction.'}},
 {'id':'Q215','title':'Personal sync host installs its transfer rule by discarding caller-supplied synthetic-address policy',
  'source':'ports/djinn/src/personal_sync.rs:start; sync/personal_sync_host.rs:open_with_blob_custody; ports/graphshell/src/personal_sync.rs:SyncSelection::with_synthetic_addresses',
  'status':'native public selector/replica counterexample reproduced; documented, not repaired',
  'evidence':'Startup installs the receipt synthetic-address rule, warning that the setter replaces rather than appends. The host subsequently calls with_synthetic_addresses([transfer_offer_rule()]) on that supplied selection, which clears the entire rule vector. An in-memory public replica refuses an unselected synthetic carrier under the supplied custom rule; composing the exact host builder chain admits and projects that carrier. A plain-address control is admitted/projected in both cases.',
  'scope':'Native public SyncSelection and PersonalGraphReplica over memory backends, using the same builder chain as the host. No full host opening or network run. Current desktop startup always selects receipt facets, masking a visible receipt-filter difference there; the caller-policy overwrite is real, but no current receipt disappearance or confidentiality breach is claimed. Synthetic carrier selection is presentation, not confidentiality.',
  'repair_triage':{'disposition':'documented in read-only audit','size':'bounded additive policy composition','reason':'Preserve caller rules when adding the host-owned transfer rule. Keep an intentional replacement API for callers that replace settings; define duplicate-prefix precedence rather than changing every setter into append. One shared composition seam should own the effective selector list.'}},
]
